1
00:00:01,880 --> 00:00:04,180
Every time you tap "order" in an app,

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,340
that tap becomes an event.

3
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:07,807
The payment service needs it.

4
00:00:07,827 --> 00:00:09,060
The warehouse needs it.

5
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:10,420
Analytics needs it.

6
00:00:10,440 --> 00:00:12,260
All of them, right now.

7
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:14,100
Wiring every service to every

8
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:16,180
other one turns into spaghetti.

9
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:17,780
So a lot of companies put

10
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:19,800
Apache Kafka in the middle.

11
00:00:20,380 --> 00:00:21,760
Hi, I'm Chris.

12
00:00:21,780 --> 00:00:23,160
I've spent over 10 years

13
00:00:23,180 --> 00:00:24,120
in data engineering

14
00:00:24,140 --> 00:00:27,000
and interviewed more than 200 engineers.

15
00:00:27,020 --> 00:00:28,760
Now I run Stackle, where I teach

16
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:31,260
the skills that actually get you hired.

17
00:00:31,717 --> 00:00:33,057
Kafka is an open-source

18
00:00:33,077 --> 00:00:35,324
platform for streaming events.

19
00:00:35,344 --> 00:00:37,217
Streaming just means data flows the moment

20
00:00:37,237 --> 00:00:39,617
it happens, not in a nightly batch.

21
00:00:39,637 --> 00:00:41,297
It started at LinkedIn, and became

22
00:00:41,317 --> 00:00:43,937
a top-level Apache project in 2012.

23
00:00:43,957 --> 00:00:45,404
Here's the core idea.

24
00:00:45,424 --> 00:00:47,617
Producers write events to a topic.

25
00:00:47,637 --> 00:00:49,457
A topic is just a named log,

26
00:00:49,477 --> 00:00:50,817
like "orders".

27
00:00:50,837 --> 00:00:52,364
New events go on the end,

28
00:00:52,384 --> 00:00:53,337
and Kafka keeps them

29
00:00:53,357 --> 00:00:54,977
for as long as you choose.

30
00:00:54,997 --> 00:00:57,350
Consumers read that log at their own pace,

31
00:00:57,370 --> 00:00:58,337
and remember their place

32
00:00:58,357 --> 00:01:00,617
with a number called an offset.

33
00:01:00,637 --> 00:01:02,097
Now, the architecture.

34
00:01:02,117 --> 00:01:03,537
A Kafka cluster is a group

35
00:01:03,557 --> 00:01:05,297
of servers called brokers.

36
00:01:05,317 --> 00:01:07,457
Each topic is split into partitions,

37
00:01:07,477 --> 00:01:08,577
spread across the brokers,

38
00:01:08,597 --> 00:01:10,657
so many machines share the load.

39
00:01:10,677 --> 00:01:12,097
Every partition is copied

40
00:01:12,117 --> 00:01:12,977
to other brokers,

41
00:01:12,997 --> 00:01:15,537
so if one fails, the data is still there.

42
00:01:15,557 --> 00:01:17,377
Consumers join a consumer group,

43
00:01:17,397 --> 00:01:18,377
and each partition

44
00:01:18,397 --> 00:01:19,590
goes to one member,

45
00:01:19,610 --> 00:01:21,777
so you scale by adding consumers.

46
00:01:21,797 --> 00:01:23,937
And a few controllers, running KRaft,

47
00:01:23,957 --> 00:01:25,297
keep track of which broker

48
00:01:25,317 --> 00:01:26,817
leads each partition.

49
00:01:26,837 --> 00:01:29,417
So when a team says, "we use Kafka,"

50
00:01:29,437 --> 00:01:30,577
it means their services

51
00:01:30,597 --> 00:01:31,857
don't call each other.

52
00:01:31,877 --> 00:01:34,137
They publish events once, and anyone who

53
00:01:34,157 --> 00:01:36,497
needs them can read them, as they happen.

54
00:01:36,517 --> 00:01:38,357
That's Apache Kafka.

55
00:01:40,417 --> 00:01:42,557
So, what should I explain next?

56
00:01:42,577 --> 00:01:43,717
Tell me in the comments below,

57
00:01:43,737 --> 00:01:45,937
and it could be the next episode.
